{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966783,"nonce":"DdSy9wPveRHTaURflywBuAEoh4g=","title":"blinc_portal_ui chart tooltip value pills read bottom-heavy — TextBaseline::Middle centers","message":"blinc_portal_ui chart tooltip value pills (\"0.642\", \"23%\") looked top-heavy with excess bottom padding. Cause: `TextBaseline::Middle` maps to `TextAnchor::Center` in blinc_gpu, which centers the FULL font em box (`ascender - descender`) around the given y. Digit-only labels carry no descenders, so the allocated descender slug sits empty at the bottom of the pill. The bias scales with the font's descender depth (~1-2px, visible at high DPI). The sparkline tooltip was worse — it used `Alphabetic` baseline placed at the pill CENTER, dropping all text into the upper half.\n\n**How to apply:** Center value pills on the CAP box, not the em box. Helper `draw_pill_value` in widget.rs draws with `TextBaseline::Alphabetic` and `baseline_y = pill_top + (pill_h + cap) * 0.5` where `cap = font_size * 0.7` (standard figure height for the UI sans stack; exact per-font cap metrics aren't reachable from the widget layer). This gives equal padding above caps and below baseline regardless of descender depth. Applied to all 7 chart tooltip sites (line/area, bar, pie, radar — both `paint_*` cores and the interactive builders — plus sparkline). For digit/number tooltips this is correct; mixed-case labels with descenders would sit slightly low but chart tooltips are numeric.","files":null}]}